<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qiang (historical people)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Qiang were pastoral and farming peoples of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, living in the regions of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, Qinghai and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>. They appear in Shang oracle bones as enemies and sacrificial victims, and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> in long frontier wars. In the 4th century a Qiang family, the Yao, founded the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> state. The modern Qiang people of Sichuan take their name from them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qiang-historical-people.jpg" alt="Qiang (historical people)" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Painting: Emperor Yuan of Liang (6th century) Photographer: undetermined · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dengzhi_ambassador_to_the_Southern_Liang_court_516-520_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Human sacrifice was practised on a large scale: oracle-bone texts record the killing of thousands of captives, many of them <b>Qiang</b>, and royal tombs contain hundreds of victims.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Meanwhile the Lesser Yuezhi, who had stayed behind in the Qilian Mountains after the great migration, lived among the <b>Qiang</b> peoples, adopted many of their customs and gradually merged with them during the Han period, so that their separate identity faded.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Yao Chang, a <b>Qiang</b> general of Former Qin, rebelled after the Fei River defeat in 383, captured and killed the Qin ruler Fu Jian in 385 and proclaimed himself emperor of Qin in 386, with his capital at Chang'an.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-qin">Later Qin</a></li><li><blockquote>Southern Liang was a frontier state of Xianbei cavalry and Chinese and <b>Qiang</b> farmers.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-liang">Southern Liang</a></li><li><blockquote>His grandson Yeyan named the people after him around 329, establishing a state that ruled the local <b>Qiang</b> herders.</blockquote><a href="/state/tuyuhun">Tuyuhun</a></li></ul></section>
